      "id": 1,
      "text": "La Vernia sits on the south bank of Cibolo Creek, where U.S. Highway 87 meets the historic town center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Vernia%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name, adopted in 1859, comes from the Spanish word for green oaks, reflecting the local oak groves that once lined the creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Vernia%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "La Vernia’s first permanent settlement began around 1850, after veterans of the Texas Revolution started moving into the area in 1837.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Vernia%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 1825 land grant from the Mexican government awarded the Cibolo Creek area to Erastus “Deaf” Smith, whose wife was a descendant of early Tejano families.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Vernia%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The La Vernia Heritage Museum showcases artifacts that trace the city’s growth from a frontier settlement to a modern community.",
